{"name":"Chain Reaction","hex":"#a4a6a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chain reaction","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical reaction where the products of the reaction influence  the reactants of a following reaction, leading to positive feedback.","A nuclear reaction in which particles produced by the fission of one atom trigger fissions of other atoms."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chain_reaction"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chain reaction","blocks":[{"text":"A chain reaction is a sequence of reactions where a reactive product or by-product causes additional reactions to take place. In a chain reaction, positive feedback leads to a self-amplifying chain of events."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_reaction","wikidata":"Q1160121"},{"title":"Chain Reaction (game show)","blocks":[{"text":"Chain Reaction is an American television game show created by Bob Stewart, in which players compete to form chains composed of two-word phrases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/32/ChainReaction2021Logo.png/330px-ChainReaction2021Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_Reaction_(game_show)","wikidata":"Q5067905"},{"title":"Chain","blocks":[{"text":"A chain is a serial assembly of connected links typically made of metal, with an overall character similar to that of a rope in that it is flexible and curved in compression, but linear, rigid, and load-bearing in tension. A chain may consist of two or more links. Chains can be classified by their design, which can be dictated by their use:"},{"items":["Those designed for lifting, such as when used with a hoist; for pulling; or for securing, such as with a bicycle lock, have links that are torus-shaped, which make the chain flexible in two dimensions. Small chains serving as jewellery are a mostly decorative analogue of such types.","Those designed for transferring power in machines have links designed to mesh with the teeth of the sprockets of the machine, and are flexible in only one dimension. They are known as roller chains, though there are also non-roller chains such as block chains."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Broad_chain_closeup.jpg/330px-Broad_chain_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain","wikidata":"Q2397485"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Kettenvergleich.jpg/500px-Kettenvergleich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Kettenvergleich.jpg/1280px-Kettenvergleich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Kettenvergleich.jpg/500px-Kettenvergleich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roller chains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKettenvergleich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg/500px-The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg/1280px-The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg/500px-The_Great_Chain_Today.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Links of the American Revolutionary War-era Hudson River Chain as a memorial at West Point","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Great_Chain_Today.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg/500px-Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg/1280px-Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg/500px-Prisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prisoner in belly chain","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrisoner_in_belly_chain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg/500px-Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg/1280px-Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg/500px-Detail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_%2847925944142%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of chain mail on samurai armor, consisting of a mesh of interlocking links. Chain mail was a common armor material around the world during the iron age, as it allowed for decent protection and mobility while usually requiring less materials and labor than plate armor. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADetail_of_Samurai_Chain-Mail_Armor_-_Gifu_Castle_Museum_-_Gifu_-_Japan_(47925944142